<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Slutsk</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Slutsk is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a> Region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>, south of Minsk. It was the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olelkovich">Olelkovich</a> princes and later of the Radziwiłłs, who made it a fortified town with schools and churches. In the eighteenth century its workshops produced the famous Slutsk sashes, woven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> belts worn by the Polish-Lithuanian nobility. It passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-partition-of-poland">Second Partition of Poland</a> in 1793.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/slutsk.jpg" alt="Slutsk" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: скульптор Сергей Оганов и архитектор Сергей Багласов · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Slutsk.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Slutsk was first mentioned in writing in 1116. It was at first part of the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turov">Turov</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pinsk">Pinsk</a> but became the capital of a separate principality in 1160, and from the 1320s or 1330s it belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>. Casimir IV Jagiellon vested it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> town rights in 1441. It was a private town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olelkovich">Olelkovich</a> and Radziwiłł families, who made it a centre of the Polish Reformed Church with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a> and a strong fortress. Scottish immigrants settled there in the middle of the 17th century.</p><p>Later the town became known for kontusz belts, among the most expensive garments of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szlachta">szlachta</a>, so that such belts worn over the żupan were often called belts of Slutsk whatever their real origin.</p><p>In 1778 it became a county seat within the Nowogródek Voivodeship, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-partition-of-poland">Second Partition of Poland</a> in 1793 it was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. Germany occupied it in 1918, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> held it in 1919 and 1920. In 1920 it was the centre of a major anti-Bolshevik uprising known as the Slutsk defence action.</p><p>The first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> are recorded in 1583, and the community was formally recognised in 1601. By 1691 Slutsk was one of the five leading communities of the Lithuanian Jewish Council. In 1750 there were 1,593 Jews, a third of the population, and three quarters of the town's merchants were Jewish. By 1897 there were 10,264 Jewish inhabitants, 77 percent of the population. The town was important for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a> study, and the Slutsk-Kletsk Yeshiva was founded there in 1883 by Rabbi Yaakov Dovid Wilovsky.</p><p>German forces occupied Slutsk on 26 June 1941. On 27 and 28 October 1941 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were taken to pits in the Gorovakha ravine and shot in what is known as the Slutsk affair, with 3,400 victims by German sources and 8,000 by Soviet ones. Thousands more were killed in 1942, and the town ghetto was liquidated on 8 February 1943. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> recaptured the town on 30 June 1944.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Slutsk lies on the Sluch River 105 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a> and is the administrative centre of Slutsk District in the Minsk Region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>. Its symbol is the woven belt, to which the Museum of the History of Slutsk Belts is dedicated.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Slutsk” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1395 Vytautas removed Volodymyr Olgerdovich from the Principality of Kiev and gave him <b>Slutsk</b> and Kapyl in compensation.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-slutsk">Duchy of Slutsk</a></li></ul></section>
